    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0122",
      "sequence": 134,
      "meaning": "Singing that glory, devotees cross the ocean of becoming; the ocean of compassion assumes a body for his people’s welfare. Rāma’s birth has many causes, each more wondrous than another; Śiva will explain one or two, and Bhavānī should listen attentively. Hari’s two beloved gatekeepers, universally known as Jaya and Vijaya, received a Brahmin curse and took dark demonic bodies as Hiraṇyakaśipu and golden-eyed Hiraṇyākṣa, world-famous destroyers of the gods’ pride. One renowned, battle-victorious warrior was slain when the Lord assumed a boar’s body; becoming Narahari, he killed the other and spread the fair fame of his devotee Prahlāda. The same mighty beings were later born as the night-roaming warriors Kumbhakarṇa and Rāvaṇa, known throughout the world as conquerors of the gods.",
