      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0124",
      "sequence": 136,
      "meaning": "Merciful Bhagavān, treasury of wondrous play, honored her curse. Through it Jalandhara became Rāvaṇa; Rāma slew him in battle and granted the supreme state. This was one cause for which Rāma assumed a human body. Poets have described many stories for each divine incarnation. On another occasion Nārada cursed the Lord, and for one kalpa he incarnated because of it. Girijā is astonished: Nārada is Viṣṇu’s devotee and a knower—why did he curse Lakṣmī’s Lord, and what offense did the Lord commit? She asks Purāri to explain this extraordinary delusion of the sage’s mind. Maheśa smiles that no one is inherently wise or foolish: each becomes exactly as Raghupati makes that person in that moment. The frame then addresses Bharadvāja and Tulsī, urging reverent hearing of Rāma’s praise, worship of Raghunātha who destroys becoming, and abandonment of pride and intoxication.",
