    {
      "entryId": "ramcharitmanas-bala-kanda-0125",
      "sequence": 137,
      "meaning": "Near an exceedingly holy cave in Himālaya flows the lovely Ganges. The divine sage Nārada delights in its beautiful, supremely pure hermitage. Contemplating mountain, river and forest, love for Ramāpati’s feet arises; remembrance of Hari arrests the momentum of a curse, and his naturally pure mind enters samādhi. Seeing the sage’s condition, Indra is frightened. He respectfully summons Kāma and sends him with helpers for Indra’s purpose. The fish-bannered god is astonished yet inwardly pleased. Indra fears that the divine sage seeks residence in his celestial city. Those lustful and greedy in the world fear everyone like crooked crows: as a foolish dog runs with a bone of pleasure at the sight of a lion, imagining it will be snatched away, so the dull king of gods feels no shame.",
